From 72f79dc513328029219314fe95f6e14adff31ca0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 17:36:17 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 061/206] feat(shorebird): FinalizeIndirectStaticCallTable --
 the DD table is built

First half of DD core. Theirs has no symbol of its own because it is
inlined into DoCompileAll; only its local DDTableBuilder leaves any
behind, and the whole function was decoded from those over rounds 3-5.

Three phases, matching their control flow:

  no mapping                       -> return    (cbz x19,  0x10021b414)
  walk, collecting dd_slot -> Code (WalkProgram with the builder)
  max_slot still -1                -> return    (tbnz #0x3f, 0x10021b474)
  retain every function the table can dispatch to, reason "DD table
      target" -- the string is theirs, at 0x1004c6567
  Array::New(max_slot + 1, kOld), array[slot] = code, store in the
      object store

The ObjectStore field lands at 0x810, the same offset theirs uses. That
is possible here and was not for Thread because the five ObjectStore
fields CheckOffsets pins all sit far earlier in the field list, so
inserting after dispatch_table_code_entries_ shifts nothing underneath
them.

Their Array::New call reads (90, len, 1) with the class id spelled out,
but that overload is protected -- what the disassembly shows is the
public two-argument New inlined into it, so ours is the same call.

One deliberate deviation, marked in the source: the builder holds zone
handles where theirs holds raw CodePtrs. Array::New in phase 3 can
allocate, and a compacting GC between capture and use would leave raw
pointers stale. This closes a hazard rather than reproducing it.

Also drops two stale comments in gen_snapshot.cc that still described
DDSlotMapping::ReadFromStream as an unimplemented stub, and the error
message that said so. The two sniff branches stay distinguishable by
message, which is load-bearing: both exit 255 on a bad file, so no test
of the sniff can rely on the exit code.

VERIFICATION -- and this is the first time any DD code has actually run,
rather than only being byte-compared:

  ninja exit 0 (Release and Product), binary newer than every source
  76 of 77 shorebird tests exit 0 (IsolateReload_ShapeChange_Const_AddSlot
      is upstream, needs --dfe, passes with it)
  verify_reproduction.sh: 24 artifacts + 14 stubs, 0 failures -- ddid
      still byte-identical on all seven fixtures, so inserting an
      ObjectStore field regressed nothing

  End to end, driving gen_snapshot with a mapping built from REAL kernel
  offsets read out of a --print_dd_function_identity_to dump:
      5-row mapping  -> "DD table target" appears 5 times, on named
                        functions, all "retained": true
      no --dd_slot_mapping -> 0 occurrences   (early return works, and
                              nothing else emits this reason)
      3-row mapping  -> 3 occurrences         (the count tracks the
                              input; it is not a constant)

Still unverified: how the table reaches Thread::indirect_static_call_table_
at run time. Nothing here has been exercised on a device.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

